he least likely entrepreneur  of all time because why would you give  something like that up um well I'll tell  you why ah  and that by the way is what we do I ate  something resembling this not exactly  this but something that probably did me  as much damage I ate I ate a chicken  sandwich and later on after that lunch  were ate the chicken sandwich I got on a  plane and I went abroad for a long  weekend but that evening I discovered  that with the chicken sandwich I'd got  some very very serious food poisoning it  was a really really nasty strain of food  poisoning which unless we've all had it  right so I won't go into the gory  details considering it's just before  lunch but over the next couple of days I  got quite seriously ill and this here is  the typhus bacteria which started to run  riot through my system so upon my return  to the UK I was hospitalised in my  condition got worse and worse and worse  and there was a moment and probably  about four or five days after I was in  hospital with kind of tubes coming in  and out of me because this bacteria had  entered my central nervous system with  doctors kind of rushing in and out  looking a bit concerned and my wife in  tears that I thought for the first time  in my rather wonderful and gilded life  that it might be nice to be dead  now I don't think I was ever actually  that close to snuffing it but I suppose  what I'd certainly had was was a moment  which started me reviewing what it was  that I wanted to do with the rest of my  life so a month or so after I started to  recover I saw it's compose in my head  and my resignation letter because I  decided that life was frankly going to  be too short to carry on working for  other people so I don't want you guys  because you are young you are the future  you're incredibly smart and brilliant  and dynamic and whilst I'd like to  employ all of you I'm now going to tell  you eight reasons why I think you should  unleash that entrepreneurial and spirit  and not wait until you are either as old  or as ill as I was reason number one  recessions are great times to start  businesses Disney IBM Apple MTV  Microsoft these are all businesses  extraordinary businesses which started  during recessions driven by creative  people with extraordinary ideas and with  real vision and determination if you  start a business in a recession and it  survives then it's no accident that it's  surviving it's because you've managed to  to create a service or a product that  genuinely responds to consumer need  you've probably got your pricing  structure spot-on and you've definitely  got a lower cost base what this means is  you will have far less competition when  the upturn eventually comes and despite  the fact that there are all sorts of  absolutely horrendous headlines going on  out there despite the fact that there's  a world were in some trouble an upturn  we'll come and when it comes there will  be a rising tide and that rising tide  will float your boat faster and higher  than everybody else's because all of the  people who decide to launch when the  Uptown comes will you'll be one or two  or three years ahead of them if you can  survive the hard times you will thrive  during the good times our business  flourished because of the recession  rather than rather than suffered on it  and it flourished because our  competitors those incumbent players the  establishment had too many problems on  their hands to snuff out the threat that  we posed to them it also meant that they  were laying off really phenomenally  talented people and that they weren't  hiring the best young talent coming  through thus leaving us a clear route to  being able to do so reason number two  the earlier you start the easier it is  the younger you are the fewer  obligations you have families are not to  be too brutal about it a collection of  lovable cost centers my old boss  Felix Dennis a man who has made a  billion pounds from media over the last  40 years or so has a great phrase for  those really costly accessories that you  accrue during your life he says if it  floats flies or fornicates ranked it  what he's done across our time he's a  single man I'm not saying he's a happy  man but he's a very rich man don't wait  until you have things to sacrifice it's  a whole lot easier to stay in the office  to win that all-important contract for  your new baby if you don't have an  actual baby back at home um and you're  also more mobile you are able to pick  your life up I know some guys who but  eight or nine years ago now decided to  go to Vietnam there are a couple of them  to set up a research business one of  them had worked in a research company in  London for three months that was the  extent of the experience and the  understanding and the knowledge that  they had they just knew more than the  people in the marketplace that they went  to they worked really hard at it it was  incredibly hard it was incredibly  exciting and they sold that business but  year and a half ago to an enormous  marketing services group for I think  about thirty million US dollars so they  did not bad out of the concept that you  can just get up and move and go and  create businesses in growth economies  China Mexico Brazil these are  phenomenally exciting and vibrant places  learn to think like an insurgent and  there are two basic fundamental  positions you can adopt in any market  first of all there's the big guy there  there's the does the incumbent and he  has the market share he has the  resources the leverage he has the  customer relationships and he has  capital and these guys are like boxing  heavyweights they are muscular they are  powerful if they catch you with a punch  it's game over but they are a bit slow  and a bit conservative they have  everything but they also have everything  to lose which sometimes makes them pause  and and then there's the insurgent they  have  nothing and nothing to lose the only way  they can win is to be nimble to be  clever to be fast to be creative to be  audacious and innovative the insurgent  is the one that always challenges the  norms challenges the established working  practices and the insurgent has nothing  to lose and it is wonderfully liberating  when you have nothing to lose you become  incredibly focused as I said earlier on  we turned around a business model that  had been going for a hundred years we  created an entirely new consumer  proposition  we created an entirely new offering for  advertisers and we created a whole new  distribution network upon which the  business seats we would never have done  that had we been the incumbents and also  what was useful about that was we knew  that if we succeeded it would be very  difficult or very painful very costly  for the incumbents to respond to that  and to change their own business model  which is exactly what's happened over  the last four years a little knowledge  is priceless and celebrate your rough  edges glory and in the fact that you  don't know everything and you especially  don't know what you can't do this this  van here represents to me  the nationwide hand distribution network  that we set up at shortlist media for  the first six months of my business my  business partner and I spent a lot of  time in vans probably around about the  same vintage as this one around the  country learning how to distribute if  we'd known how difficult it was going to  be we never have started  and we'd still be absolutely at square  one we eventually did it and it is now  our most valuable asset of all talking  about it's your greatest asset as a  young person is that you understand  young people I employ young people  because they know what's going on um  it's a phenomenally effective and  cost-effective way for me to run my  business I'm telling you this so that  you don't fall into the same trap and  your greatest asset is that you know how  young people behave and why they behave  that way you understand why it 